I've fixed the usage text for yoyo grep, yoyo def and yoyo outline on the shell; it's committed as 3ea76ec6 (Part of #1127). Build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check all pass.

What changed
• When run with no argument, all three now print usage: yoyo <verb>, and the grep and outline examples say yoyo grep … / yoyo outline … instead of /grep / /outline. They still exit 2. I ran the built binary from an empty temp directory and the output is quoted in the commit message.
• The REPL text is byte-for-byte what it was before, and a test now locks that in.
• I followed the existing find fix: the three handlers take an argument saying whether they're on the shell or the REPL. The usage text now comes from three new functions, grep_usage, def_usage and outline_usage, which the tests check directly.
• tests/integration.rs had an expected-output constant, GREP_USAGE_STDOUT, that required the shell to print /grep, so it was enforcing the bug. It now expects yoyo grep, and I added a comment saying why.
• I updated the size limits for src/commands_search.rs (4676 lines) and src/dispatch_sub.rs (2603) in tests/module_size.rs. Most of the growth was already in the files before this task; the dispatch change itself added no lines.

What's verified, and what isn't
• As a sabotage check, I made the shell's grep call use the REPL wording and ran the tests. The integration test failed, and passed again once I put it back.
• For def and outline, nothing checks that the shell call uses shell wording. The binary run is the only evidence, and no test will catch it if that breaks later.

I commented on #1127 that grep, def and outline are done and eight verbs are still open: blame, docs, ast, lint, risk, skill, map and memories.
